[metadata]
title: iPhone Timelapse With Reelapse: RAW, Holy Grail & Slow Shutter
channel: @octopusinc_
duration: 15:30
source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9hRlTnRTfKA
upload_date: 20260721
transcript_source: captions+frames
route: visual

---

[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Night city street with light trails, "REELAPSE" logo on screen.
AUDIO: 'If you\'ve tried capturing time-lapse on your&nbsp; iPhone using the native camera app, you\'ve&nbsp;&nbsp; probably been disappointed with the results.&nbsp; Flickering exposure, white balance shifts, jarring&nbsp;&nbsp; motion, zero manual controls, and ultimately hours&nbsp; wasted capturing footage you can\'t even use. But&nbsp;&nbsp; the app Reelapse fixes all of that and takes the&nbsp; guesswork out of creating stunning time-lapse on&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'iPhone. And with the release of version 2.1, which&nbsp; adds full support for Raw time-lapse capture,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can now rival traditional cameras&nbsp; for incredible quality. In this tutorial,&nbsp;&nbsp; I\'ll show you how to create stunning time-lapses&nbsp; on your iPhone, from the best settings and slow&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shutter effects through to raw holy grail&nbsp; time-lapse. You\'ll learn everything you need&nbsp;&nbsp; to get up and running with this app. Thanks to&nbsp; Reeflex for sponsoring this tutorial. The app is&nbsp;&nbsp; linked in the pinned comment. Now, let\'s dive&nbsp; in and learn to capture our first time-lapse&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:46] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse settings for Video Codec, with HEVC, ProRes 422 HQ, ProRes 422, and ProRes 422 LT options.
AUDIO: 'with relapse. Before starting a time-lapse in&nbsp; any mode, open settings and select your frame&nbsp;&nbsp; rate. I\'m choosing 24 fps. It\'s the standard for&nbsp; motion picture capture, and a lower frame rate&nbsp;&nbsp; always means less waiting for your time-lapse to&nbsp; finish. Tap back. Next, tap video codec. If your&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'phone supports ProRes, you can choose it here for&nbsp; higher quality 4:2:2 all intra compression. ProRes&nbsp;&nbsp; 4:2:2 is ideal but for smaller file sizes or if&nbsp; your phone doesn\'t support ProRes, choose HEVC,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tap back, then tap bit rate and choose 100&nbsp; megabits per second for highest quality. The&nbsp;&nbsp; last option is backup. Save backup video saves a&nbsp; duplicate to files in addition to photos. Ideal&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:29] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse interface with "CAPTURE" and "OUTPUT" times, and controls for "FRAMES", "INTERVAL", and "EFFECTS".
AUDIO: 'if you\'re prone to accidentally deleting videos&nbsp; from the photos app. Save individual frame saves&nbsp;&nbsp; individual JPEGs alongside your video. I\'d leave&nbsp; both off if you\'re not sure you need them. Tap the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'camera icon to return to the main interface. With&nbsp; settings configured, let\'s set up the time-lapse&nbsp;&nbsp; itself using the time-lapse dashboard. It takes&nbsp; the guesswork out of time-lapse by letting you&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'enter total frame count, interval, and slow&nbsp; shutter effects and automatically calculating&nbsp;&nbsp; your capture session length and final video output&nbsp; time. By default, we\'re capturing 24 frames at a&nbsp;&nbsp; half second interval with slow shutter effects&nbsp; off. A 12 second wait for a 1second finished&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:11] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse interface, adjusting the "INTERVAL" slider.
AUDIO: 'time-lapse. Not enough. So, let\'s configure it&nbsp; properly. Tap frames. Increase the frame count&nbsp;&nbsp; and you\'ll see capture time and output both grow&nbsp; with it. Reduce it and both shrink. Next, tap&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interval. As you increase the interval, which is&nbsp; the time between each frame captured, only capture&nbsp;&nbsp; time grows. Output stays fixed. The real impact&nbsp; of interval is on the compression of time in your&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'finished time-lapse, and it\'s easier to show than&nbsp; explain. On the left is a 1second interval, in&nbsp;&nbsp; the middle a 5-second interval, and on the right&nbsp; a 15-second interval. The longer the interval,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the more dramatic the compression of motion&nbsp; in the scene, but the longer the capture time&nbsp;&nbsp; needed to get the same number of frames. The last&nbsp; item in the dashboard is effects. Computational&nbsp;&nbsp; slow shutter simulations for motion blur and light&nbsp; trails, replicating what you\'d normally get from a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:05] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse interface, adjusting "EFFECTS" for motion blur.
AUDIO: 'physical ND filter combined with slow shutter&nbsp; speeds. Increase the motion blur effect and&nbsp;&nbsp; you\'ll see capture time increase further without&nbsp; affecting the final output. The beauty of Reelapse&nbsp;&nbsp; is that this dashboard removes all the guesswork,&nbsp; calculating capture and output in real time as you&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'adjust frames, interval, and effects. You\'ll know&nbsp; exactly how long you\'ll be freezing your ass off&nbsp;&nbsp; to get your final time-lapse duration. If you\'re&nbsp; wondering what the best interval is, there isn\'t&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one, only the best interval for your scene, and&nbsp; you\'ll only learn that with practice. As a general&nbsp;&nbsp; guide, for slow moving scenes like cloudscapes, a&nbsp; longer interval creates more dynamic motion. For a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:46] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse interface, demonstrating the "EFFECTS" slider.
AUDIO: 'time-lapse shot from a moving vehicle where the&nbsp; environment is changing rapidly, a much shorter&nbsp;&nbsp; interval around half a second paired with a half&nbsp; second of motion blur works well. For this scene,&nbsp;&nbsp; I\'ll choose a 10-second interval. Capture 192&nbsp; frames and apply 1 second of motion blur for a&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'final 8 second recording. But before we hit the&nbsp; shutter, there are two more things to set. The&nbsp;&nbsp; first is our capture mode. And Reelapse offers&nbsp; three capture modes. Max captures using your&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sensor\'s full width and height, delivering 4K&nbsp; video in your codec of choice at 4032x3024, more&nbsp;&nbsp; commonly known as open gate. This gives you more&nbsp; information to reframe for 16x9 in post or you can&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:31] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse interface, with "4K" mode selected.
AUDIO: 'publish natively in 4x3. Tap the mode picker&nbsp; to enter 4K mode. This crops and renders your&nbsp;&nbsp; time-lapse in 16x9 at 3840x 2160. The standard&nbsp; aspect ratio for web and TV. A great choice if you&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t want to reframe in post and plan to publish&nbsp; in 16x9. The only difference between them is the&nbsp;&nbsp; output resolution. Tap the mode picker again&nbsp; to enter RAW mode. Brand new in Relapse version&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2.1. RAW unlocks unrivalled image quality and&nbsp; editing flexibility by bypassing the image signal&nbsp;&nbsp; processing used in the standard photo stream&nbsp; in max and 4K modes. Instead, saving a DNG raw&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'image sequence that you can turn into a time-lapse&nbsp; movie in post. Because these are RAW images, you&nbsp;&nbsp; can\'t use motion blur effects and file sizes are&nbsp; significantly larger. In return, you get the most&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:24] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse interface, with "RAW" mode selected.
AUDIO: 'natural looking image, giving you the ability to&nbsp; dial in the perfect look in post using an app like&nbsp;&nbsp; Lightroom or Resolve, recover clipped highlights,&nbsp; and even achieve holy grail day to night&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'time-lapse using the industry standard LRTimelapse&nbsp; application for Mac and Windows. While I\'d&nbsp;&nbsp; recommend RAW for the best results, for now, I\'ll&nbsp; choose Max so the video is created in Reelapse.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Later in this tutorial, I\'ll show you how to&nbsp; harness the power of RAW in Reelapse. But for now,&nbsp;&nbsp; with modes set, the only thing left before&nbsp; starting is to set and lock focus, exposure,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:01] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse interface, with white balance locked (green "WB" value).
AUDIO: 'and white balance. By default, focus, exposure,&nbsp; and white balance are in full auto. You could just&nbsp;&nbsp; tap the shutter and start your time-lapse, but&nbsp; that will result in distracting flicker that hurts&nbsp;&nbsp; the final quality. For best results, set each of&nbsp; these manually. To lock the current white balance&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'value, just tap it. Once a value turns green,&nbsp; it\'s locked and won\'t change. Use the slider to&nbsp;&nbsp; manually override the locked automatic value and&nbsp; double tap in the preview to reset any lock value&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'back to auto. I\'ll tap white balance again to&nbsp; lock the current value so it doesn\'t shift during&nbsp;&nbsp; recording. Next, tap in the live preview to set&nbsp; your focus point. Then tap MF to lock it. Tap to&nbsp;&nbsp; focus won\'t always work perfectly, especially&nbsp; in low light or on low contrast areas. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:47] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse interface, with histogram and exposure controls visible.
AUDIO: 'use the manual focus slider to dial it in. Tap&nbsp; the eye icon to engage focus peaking, which makes&nbsp;&nbsp; this easier. Then, disable peaking once focus is&nbsp; set. Lastly, tap to lock exposure. Tap either ISO&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or shutter speed and the other locks with it.&nbsp; You can also make manual adjustments using the&nbsp;&nbsp; sliders. For best results, enable the histogram&nbsp; and zebras to spot any clipping and adjust using&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the manual ISO and shutter speed sliders as&nbsp; needed. Once exposure is set and locked, disable&nbsp;&nbsp; them both so you can view capture and output time&nbsp; again. Then press the shutter button, sit back,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and enjoy a good book until your time-lapse&nbsp; capture is complete. As mentioned previously,&nbsp;&nbsp; Reelapse makes available two slow shutter modes&nbsp; in max and 4K modes, motion blur and light trails.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:39] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Timelapse footage showing motion blur effect over a body of water with a rainbow.
AUDIO: 'And they are so cool, it\'s worth exploring&nbsp; them in detail. Now, you might be wondering,&nbsp;&nbsp; what is the difference between these two modes?&nbsp; And the answer is the frame stacking algorithms&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'used to simulate long exposure. The motion blur&nbsp; mode computes an optimized temporal average across&nbsp;&nbsp; frames and is ideal for dynamic scenes like cars&nbsp; zooming by, clouds drifting across the sky, and&nbsp;&nbsp; even for removing objects from the scene. Whereas&nbsp; the light trails pipeline selectively preserves&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and accumulates only the brightest pixels over&nbsp; time and is best suited for light trails. Neither&nbsp;&nbsp; algorithm comes from a public Apple API. Both&nbsp; were developed in-house by the Reeflex engineers&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:20] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Presenter standing outdoors with an iPhone mounted on a tripod with an external lens.
AUDIO: 'and run entirely on the devices GPU to maximize&nbsp; frame rate and keep energy usage low. And the&nbsp;&nbsp; best feature of both slow shutter modes is that&nbsp; you get to preview the effect in real time during&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capture. Simply select the effect duration and&nbsp; tap the shutter and you can instantly see how it&nbsp;&nbsp; will look. If it doesn\'t look right, just stop the&nbsp; timelapse and adjust the duration, then try again.&nbsp;&nbsp; The most powerful feature in Reelapse is the brand&nbsp; new RAW mode introduced in version 2.1. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rendering HEVC or ProRes video in-app, Reelapse&nbsp; saves a folder full of raw DNG photos. And that&nbsp;&nbsp; does a few very important things. First, it&nbsp; bypasses Apple\'s image signal processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'giving you an untouched image with maximum&nbsp; flexibility to edit in post. Second, working with&nbsp;&nbsp; RAW images lets you restore clipped highlights&nbsp; even when clipping two or three RGB channels for&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:15] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Presenter speaking to the camera, with iPhone and external lens on a tripod in the background.
AUDIO: 'unbelievable dynamic range. Third, because you\'re&nbsp; capturing individual RAW images, you can pull any&nbsp;&nbsp; frame out of a sequence and edit it as a high&nbsp; quality standalone photo, something that isn\'t&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'possible when working with a compressed HEVC or&nbsp; ProRes video output. And lastly, RAW time-lapse&nbsp;&nbsp; makes holy grail time-lapse possible. Seamlessly&nbsp; transitioning between day and night using the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'industry standard application LRTimelapse.&nbsp; Raw capture in Reelapse is almost identical to&nbsp;&nbsp; time-lapse capture using the max or 4K modes with&nbsp; the only difference being effects are unavailable.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Just tap the mode picker to enter RAW mode.&nbsp; Then set your interval and number of frames. Then set and lock white balance, focus,&nbsp; and exposure, and tap the shutter button,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and you\'re away. When finished, Relapse&nbsp; will save a video render to photo so you&nbsp;&nbsp; can quickly preview the results. The full&nbsp; quality raw image sequence is saved under&nbsp;&nbsp; iOS files on my iPhone. Reelapse. To&nbsp; turn this into a finished time-lapse,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just import this folder onto your Mac or PC. You&nbsp; can edit the images in an app like Lightroom,&nbsp;&nbsp; export them, and use QuickTime to open an&nbsp; image sequence and convert it to a movie.'

[10:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'My preferred workflow is to use DaVinci Resolve,&nbsp; which is free. From the media page, import your&nbsp;&nbsp; image sequence folder. Resolve automatically&nbsp; treats it as a video. Then under the Color page,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:50] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve interface, showing color grading nodes and Filmbox Pro settings.
AUDIO: 'you can utilize Resolve\'s camera raw tools to&nbsp; restore clipped highlights and make adjustments&nbsp;&nbsp; to exposure and white balance. Use a color space&nbsp; transform to convert it into a color space and&nbsp;&nbsp; gamma of your choosing and then grade it as you&nbsp; would any other video clip using either LUTs&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or film emulation plugins like Filmbox Pro. And&nbsp; then export your finished video ready to share.&nbsp;&nbsp; To capture a holy grail time-lapse in Reelapse,&nbsp; enter RAW mode and set your interval long enough&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that you have time to adjust exposure between&nbsp; captures. I\'m choosing a 15-second interval.&nbsp;&nbsp; Then I\'ll increase my frame count to cover&nbsp; a 3-hour capture time, 1 hour before sunset,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:32] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: iPhone screen showing Reelapse interface in RAW mode, with histogram and exposure controls.
AUDIO: '1 hour of sunset, and 1 hour after sunset into&nbsp; night. The next step is the most important.&nbsp;&nbsp; Enable the histogram and monitor it throughout&nbsp; the capture. Then set and lock your white balance,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'focus, and exposure. For exposure, I\'ll set ISO&nbsp; nice and low, then refine with shutter speed over&nbsp;&nbsp; the course of my time-lapse. Now, hit the shutter&nbsp; button. As time passes, you\'ll need to readjust&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exposure to account for the drop in light, which&nbsp; is exactly why a long interval is essential. It&nbsp;&nbsp; gives you enough time to complete the adjustment&nbsp; before the next photo is captured. We will smooth&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out the exposure spikes later in the edit.&nbsp; And if you make it through the full capture&nbsp;&nbsp; without your phone getting stolen or dying from&nbsp; frostbite, the real magic happens in post using&nbsp;&nbsp; LR Time-lapse in combination with Adobe Lightroom.&nbsp; LR Time Lapse is the industry standard application&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for holy grail on Mac and Windows. Simply add&nbsp; your relapse raw image sequence and LRTimelapse&nbsp;&nbsp; will generate a luminance graph. Every spike here&nbsp; is where I manually adjusted exposure. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this creates jarring exposure jumps that will ruin&nbsp; our time-lapse if left untreated. To fix this,&nbsp;&nbsp; work through the symbol flow that runs from&nbsp; left to right at the top of the interface.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Starting with the key frame wizard. This&nbsp; will automatically generate recommended key&nbsp;&nbsp; frames for image adjustment. Then click holy&nbsp; grail wizard, which will equalize the jumps&nbsp;&nbsp; in brightness introduced by ramping our exposure&nbsp; during capture. Save this to the XMP metadata. And&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:04] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Adobe Lightroom Classic interface, showing the "Scripts" menu with LRTimelapse options.
AUDIO: 'now for the fun part. Drag and drop your sequence&nbsp; into Lightroom Classic. And with ads selected,&nbsp;&nbsp; click to import your sequence. LR Timelapse&nbsp; installs a filter in Lightroom to show key&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'frame images only. Open the first image in the&nbsp; develop module. Then edit it until you\'re happy.&nbsp;&nbsp; Then shift select the next key frame image&nbsp; and from the scripts menu, select sync key&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'frames. This will copy your previous edits across.&nbsp; Make further adjustments as needed. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp; you may wish to adjust the white balance and&nbsp; refine exposure. Then repeat the sync key frames&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'process to your next image. Make any necessary&nbsp; adjustments until all key frames are edited and&nbsp;&nbsp; to your liking. Press G to return to the grid&nbsp; view. Select all images. Click on the metadata&nbsp;&nbsp; menu and choose save metadata to files and return&nbsp; to LR time-lapse. Then click auto transition and&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'LRtimelapse will interpolate changes across all&nbsp; images based on the key frames you just edited&nbsp;&nbsp; to smoothly transition from start to end. As you&nbsp; can see, despite the exposure curve being much&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'smoother, we still get jumps which will result&nbsp; in flicker. To fix this, the last step is to&nbsp;&nbsp; click on visual Deflicker and smooth your exposure&nbsp; curve. You can enable additional passes for even&nbsp;&nbsp; smoother results. Then click apply. As you can&nbsp; see, LR Time-lapse runs through the sequence and&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'smooths out all jumps. Then, it\'s as simple as&nbsp; exporting your finished movie from LRTimelapse&nbsp;&nbsp; in HEVC or ProRes. And we\'ve taken our iPhone&nbsp; time-lapse from humdrum to holy grail in just&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:41] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Presenter speaking to the camera, with iPhone and external lens on a tripod in the background.
AUDIO: 'a few simple steps for results previously only&nbsp; possible on dedicated mirrorless and DSLR cameras.&nbsp;&nbsp; So whether you\'re capturing time-lapse in max&nbsp; and 4K modes with motion blur effects or in RAW&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mode for the highest image quality possible,&nbsp; you now know how to master the fundamentals&nbsp;&nbsp; of time-lapse using relapse. I hope this video&nbsp; helped you capture better time-lapse. If it did,&nbsp;&nbsp; please like, share, and subscribe to the channel,&nbsp; and let me know if you have any questions in the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comments below. My name\'s Elliot. Thanks for&nbsp; watching. I\'ll see you in the next one. Bye.'
